vector.library/AddEllipse                            vector.library/AddEllipse

   NAME
	AddEllipse -- A closed ellipse round (cx, cy); SVG's ellipse.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AddEllipse(vb: *VectorBase, path: *vector.Path, cx: f32, cy: f32, rx: f32, ry: f32) void

   SINCE
	1.1. LVO -80.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path.
	- `cx`, `cy` - its centre, in the path's units.
	- `rx`, `ry` - its radii across and down; the same for a circle.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	A contour of its own, closed, of four cubic curves, as SVG draws an
	`ellipse` or a `circle`: from its rightmost point, clockwise on the
	screen. A radius of 0 or less adds nothing. The current point is the
	rightmost point afterwards.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The caller's, as before.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AddRect`, `ArcTo`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.AddEllipse(path, 50, 50, 20, 20); // a circle
vector.library/AddPathData                          vector.library/AddPathData

   NAME
	AddPathData -- SVG path data onto the path: VECTORERR_SYNTAX at the first error, the path kept up to it.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AddPathData(vb: *VectorBase, path: *vector.Path, text: [*]const u8, length: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.1. LVO -84.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path the contours are added to.
	- `text`, `length` - the data: the `d` of an SVG `path`, such as
	  `M 10 10 h 20 a 5 5 0 0 1 5 5 z`. It need not end in a 0.

   RESULT
	`VECTOR_OK`; `VECTORERR_SYNTAX` at the first thing that is not SVG's
	path data; `VECTORERR_NOMEM` when a segment did not fit.

   BEHAVIOR
	Every command of SVG's path data, in both its forms: absolute, and
	relative to the current point. `M`, `L`, `H`, `V`, `C`, `S`, `Q`,
	`T`, `A`, `Z`; a command's numbers given again for another of the
	same - a move's as lines - and `S` and `T` mirroring the last curve's
	control point when the command before was one of their kind. Numbers
	may run together where a sign or a second point parts them
	(`10-5.5.5`), with an exponent, and an arc's two flags may be single
	digits with nothing between them.

	At an error the commands before it are kept and the one it is in is
	not, which is how SVG draws data that goes wrong. The data is a path
	of its own: it must start with a move, and a relative one first is
	taken from (0, 0).

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The text is the caller's and only read.

   NOTES
	A program can keep its pictures as path data and build them with
	this, as an SVG file does.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`ArcTo`, `MoveTo`, `PathBounds`

   EXAMPLES
	    const d = "M 4 12 l 6 6 l 10 -12";
	    if (vb.AddPathData(path, d, d.len) != vector.VECTOR_OK) return;
vector.library/AddRect                                  vector.library/AddRect

   NAME
	AddRect -- A closed rectangle, its corners rounded rx across and ry down; SVG's rect.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AddRect(vb: *VectorBase, path: *vector.Path, rect: *const vector.Rect, rx: f32, ry: f32) void

   SINCE
	1.1. LVO -76.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path.
	- `rect` - where it is and how large (`vector.Rect`), in the path's
	  units.
	- `rx`, `ry` - how far round each corner goes, across and down; 0 for
	  square corners.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	A contour of its own, closed, as SVG draws a `rect`: from the top
	left - after its rounding - going right, so clockwise on the screen.
	The corners are quarters of an ellipse, `rx` and `ry` each held to
	half the side it is along, and square when either is 0 or less. A
	width or a height of 0 or less adds nothing. The current point is the
	contour's start afterwards.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The rectangle is the caller's and only read.

   NOTES
	SVG's `rx` given alone stands for `ry` too, and the other way round:
	a program reading SVG passes the one for both.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`AddEllipse`, `MoveTo`

   EXAMPLES
	    const box = vector.Rect{ .x = 10, .y = 10, .width = 80, .height = 40 };
	    vb.AddRect(path, &box, 6, 6);
vector.library/AddSegments                          vector.library/AddSegments

   NAME
	AddSegments -- Segments onto the path in one call: a verb each (SEG_*) and the points they take, in order; VECTORERR_SYNTAX at a verb it does not know or short of points, the path kept up to it.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn AddSegments(vb: *VectorBase, path: *vector.Path, verbs: [*]const u8, verb_count: u32, points: [*]const vector.Point, point_count: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.2. LVO -100.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path the segments are added to.
	- `verbs`, `verb_count` - a byte a segment: `SEG_MOVE`, `SEG_LINE`,
	  `SEG_QUAD`, `SEG_CUBIC` or `SEG_CLOSE`.
	- `points`, `point_count` - the points the verbs take, one verb's
	  after another's: a move and a line one, a quadratic curve two (its
	  control point, then its end), a cubic one three (its two control
	  points, then its end), a close none.

   RESULT
	`VECTOR_OK`; `VECTORERR_SYNTAX` at a verb that is none of these or
	that has fewer points left than it takes; `VECTORERR_NOMEM` when a
	segment did not fit.

   BEHAVIOR
	The path comes out as the same calls one at a time - `MoveTo`,
	`LineTo`, `QuadTo`, `CubicTo`, `ClosePath` - would make it, and is
	added to what it holds already: a segment with no move before it is
	passed over, and one after a close starts where the closed contour
	began. The room for all of it is taken at once.

	At an error the segments before it are kept. Points past the ones
	the last verb takes are not looked at.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The verbs and the points are the caller's and only read; the path
	keeps copies.

   NOTES
	For a path a program keeps as data, or reads out of a file: one call
	through the jump table for all of it, rather than one a segment.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`MoveTo`, `AddPathData`, `ClearPath`

   EXAMPLES
	    const verbs = [_]u8{ vector.SEG_MOVE, vector.SEG_LINE, vector.SEG_LINE, vector.SEG_CLOSE };
	    const points = [_]vector.Point{ .{ .x = 0, .y = 0 }, .{ .x = 10, .y = 0 }, .{ .x = 5, .y = 8 } };
	    if (vb.AddSegments(path, &verbs, verbs.len, &points, points.len) != vector.VECTOR_OK) return;
vector.library/ArcTo                                      vector.library/ArcTo

   NAME
	ArcTo -- SVG's elliptical arc from the current point to (x, y): the radii, the ellipse's angle in degrees, ARC_LARGE and ARC_SWEEP.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn ArcTo(vb: *VectorBase, path: *vector.Path, rx: f32, ry: f32, angle: f32, flags: u32, x: f32, y: f32) void

   SINCE
	1.1. LVO -72.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path.
	- `rx`, `ry` - the ellipse's radii across and down, in the path's
	  units; their sign is taken off.
	- `angle` - how far the ellipse is turned, in degrees, clockwise on
	  the screen.
	- `flags` - `ARC_LARGE` for the arc of more than half the ellipse
	  rather than less, `ARC_SWEEP` for the one going the way angles rise
	  (clockwise on the screen) rather than the other.
	- `x`, `y` - where the arc ends.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	SVG's `A`: of the two ellipses of these radii and this angle through
	the current point and (x, y), and the two arcs of each between them,
	the one the flags pick. Radii too small to reach from one end to the
	other are made just large enough, so the arc is half an ellipse; a
	radius of 0 makes a line, and an arc ending where it starts is
	nothing. It is added as a cubic curve for every quarter turn or less
	of it, kept as curves until drawn. Without a current point it is
	passed over, and after a `ClosePath` it starts a new contour, as
	`LineTo` does.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The caller's, as before.

   NOTES
	Without the memory for it the arc is added as far as it fitted, a
	curve at a time, and the path remembers that something did not fit
	(see `MoveTo`).

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`CubicTo`, `AddEllipse`, `AddPathData`

   EXAMPLES
	    // A half circle of radius 10 over the top from (0, 10) to (20, 10).
	    vb.MoveTo(path, 0, 10);
	    vb.ArcTo(path, 10, 10, 0, vector.ARC_SWEEP, 20, 10);
vector.library/BeginMask                              vector.library/BeginMask

   NAME
	BeginMask -- What is drawn until EndMask makes a mask rather than being seen.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn BeginMask(vb: *VectorBase, canvas: *vector.Canvas) i32

   SINCE
	1.3. LVO -112.

   INPUTS
	- `canvas` - the canvas.

   RESULT
	`VECTOR_OK`; `VECTORERR_NOMEM` without the memory for the mask's
	layer; `VECTORERR_DEPTH` past `MAX_LAYERS` layers one inside another.
	Whatever it answers, an `EndMask` goes with it, and a `PopClip` with
	that.

   BEHAVIOR
	A layer is pushed, clear, that is never put down: every fill and
	stroke until the `EndMask` that goes with it is drawn onto it, not
	clipped by the clips already pushed, and `EndMask` turns it into a
	clip. Layers, clips and masks may be pushed and popped inside it, as
	anywhere.

	Without the layer's memory, or past the most layers, what is drawn
	until `EndMask` is drawn nowhere, and the mask clips nothing.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The layer is the canvas's, given back by `EndMask` or by
	`DisposeCanvas`.

   NOTES
	The layer is four bytes a pixel of the whole canvas until it ends,
	and the mask then one.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`EndMask`, `PopClip`, `PushClip`, `PushLayer`

   EXAMPLES
	    _ = vb.BeginMask(canvas);
	    _ = vb.FillPath(canvas, fade, null, &white_to_black, vector.RULE_NONZERO);
	    _ = vb.EndMask(canvas, vector.MASK_LUMINANCE);
	    _ = vb.FillPath(canvas, picture, null, &red, vector.RULE_NONZERO);
	    vb.PopClip(canvas);
vector.library/BlurLayer                              vector.library/BlurLayer

   NAME
	BlurLayer -- What is drawn on the last layer pushed, blurred.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn BlurLayer(vb: *VectorBase, canvas: *vector.Canvas, deviation_x: f32, deviation_y: f32) i32

   SINCE
	1.4. LVO -120.

   INPUTS
	- `canvas` - the canvas.
	- `deviation_x` - the blur's standard deviation across, in pixels.
	- `deviation_y` - and down. 0, less, or not a number is no blur that
	  way.

   RESULT
	- `VECTOR_OK` - blurred, or there was nothing to blur.
	- `VECTORERR_DEPTH` - no layer is pushed.
	- `VECTORERR_NOMEM` - the layer had no memory of its own, so what was
	  drawn on it is in what is under it already; or there was not the
	  memory for a row of the work. Nothing is blurred.

   BEHAVIOR
	The layer's pens are blurred in place, as SVG's `feGaussianBlur` is
	made: three box blurs one after another, across and then down, each
	of the size SVG gives for the deviation - within a few per cent of a
	Gaussian, and costing the same whatever its size. They are blurred
	premultiplied, so a clear pixel's colour goes nowhere. What is past
	the canvas's edge is clear, and what is blurred past it is lost. The
	layer is then put down by `PopLayer` as any other, at its opacity and
	through the clips under it: a clip pushed before the layer cuts the
	blur, as SVG's filter region does.

	A mask being drawn (`BeginMask`) is a layer too, and blurs the same:
	a mask with a soft edge. A layer past the most, or one inside a mask
	begun past them, is drawn nowhere, and `VECTOR_OK` is answered.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The layer is the canvas's; a row's work is allocated and given back
	before the call returns.

   BUGS
	Blurred in the pens' colours as they are (sRGB), which SVG asks for
	with `color-interpolation-filters: sRGB`; its default, linearRGB,
	blurs two colours' edge a little lighter. The deviations are the
	pixels' across and down: a picture drawn turned is blurred along the
	canvas's rows and columns, not its own.

   SEE ALSO
	`PushLayer`, `PopLayer`, `PushClip`

   EXAMPLES
	    _ = vb.PushLayer(canvas, 1);
	    _ = vb.FillPath(canvas, path, &matrix, &paint, sdk.vector.RULE_NONZERO);
	    _ = vb.BlurLayer(canvas, 4, 4);
	    vb.PopLayer(canvas);
vector.library/ClearPath                              vector.library/ClearPath

   NAME
	ClearPath -- The path emptied, its memory kept for the next one built in it.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn ClearPath(vb: *VectorBase, path: *vector.Path) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -28.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	Every contour goes, and with it the current point and a want of
	memory the path had met: it is as `CreatePath` made it, but it keeps
	the memory it had grown to. A program that builds a path anew for
	every frame clears one rather than making and giving back another.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The caller's, as before.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`CreatePath`, `DisposePath`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.ClearPath(path);
	    vb.MoveTo(path, 0, 0);
vector.library/ClosePath                              vector.library/ClosePath

   NAME
	ClosePath -- The contour closed back to where it started.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn ClosePath(vb: *VectorBase, path: *vector.Path) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -48.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	A straight line back to the contour's start closes it: a stroke joins
	its ends there instead of giving them caps. The current point is the
	start from then on, and the next segment starts a new contour there.
	A contour already closed, or no contour at all, is left as it is.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The caller's, as before.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`MoveTo`, `StrokePath`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.MoveTo(path, 0, 0);
	    vb.LineTo(path, 10, 0);
	    vb.LineTo(path, 10, 10);
	    vb.ClosePath(path);
vector.library/CreateCanvasTagList          vector.library/CreateCanvasTagList

   NAME
	CreateCanvasTagList -- A canvas over the caller's pens (VCA_*); null without a size or the memory.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn CreateCanvasTagList(vb: *VectorBase, tags: ?[*]const utility.TagItem) ?*vector.Canvas

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -52.

   INPUTS
	- `tags` - what the canvas is:
	  - `VCA_Width`, `VCA_Height` - its size in pixels. Both are needed,
	    each from 1 to 1048576.
	  - `VCA_Pens` - `[*]graphics.Pen`, what is drawn on: 0xAARRGGBB, not
	    premultiplied. Without it nothing is drawn until `SetCanvasPens`.
	  - `VCA_BytesPerRow` - from one row of pens to the next; four times
	    the width unless given.

   RESULT
	The canvas, or null: no size, or not the memory for its working
	memory.

   BEHAVIOR
	The canvas holds the library's working memory for drawing on pens of
	that size: the coverage of a band of rows, at most 512 KiB of it,
	which a fill or a stroke is summed into a band at a time. A canvas of
	360 by 360 pixels is one band; a larger one is drawn in as many as it
	takes. The pens themselves are only written, where a shape covers
	them.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The canvas is the caller's until `DisposeCanvas`, and one task's at a
	time. The pens stay the caller's, and must last while they are drawn
	on.

   NOTES
	The tags are read here and not kept. `TAG_IGNORE`, `TAG_MORE` and
	`TAG_SKIP` are followed as utility.library follows them.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`DisposeCanvas`, `SetCanvasPens`, `FillPath`, `StrokePath`

   EXAMPLES
	    const tags = [_]utility.TagItem{
	        .{ .tag = vector.VCA_Width, .data = 48 },
	        .{ .tag = vector.VCA_Height, .data = 48 },
	        .{ .tag = vector.VCA_Pens, .data = @intFromPtr(&pens) },
	        .{},
	    };
	    const canvas = vb.CreateCanvasTagList(&tags) orelse return error.NoMemory;
	    defer vb.DisposeCanvas(canvas);
vector.library/CreatePath                            vector.library/CreatePath

   NAME
	CreatePath -- An empty path; null without the memory for one.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn CreatePath(vb: *VectorBase) ?*vector.Path

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -20.

   INPUTS
	None.

   RESULT
	The path, or null when there was not the memory for it.

   BEHAVIOR
	A path holds contours of lines and curves in the caller's own units,
	built a segment at a time with `MoveTo`, `LineTo`, `QuadTo`, `CubicTo`
	and `ClosePath`. Curves stay curves: the path is broken into lines
	only when it is drawn, at the size it is drawn at, so one path is
	drawn at any size. Its memory grows as segments are added.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The caller's until `DisposePath`, and one task's at a time: the
	library holds no lock for it.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`DisposePath`, `ClearPath`, `MoveTo`, `FillPath`, `StrokePath`

   EXAMPLES
	    const path = vb.CreatePath() orelse return error.NoMemory;
	    defer vb.DisposePath(path);
vector.library/CubicTo                                  vector.library/CubicTo

   NAME
	CubicTo -- A cubic curve from the current point to (x, y), leaving toward (x1, y1) and arriving from (x2, y2).

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn CubicTo(vb: *VectorBase, path: *vector.Path, x1: f32, y1: f32, x2: f32, y2: f32, x: f32, y: f32) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -44.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path.
	- `x1`, `y1` - the first control point, in the path's units.
	- `x2`, `y2` - the second.
	- `x`, `y` - where the curve ends.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	A Bézier curve, as SVG's `C` and Lottie's vertices draw: (x, y) is the
	current point from then on. It is kept as a curve and broken into
	lines only when drawn, enough of them that none strays from it by
	more than a tenth of a pixel at the size it is drawn at (a quarter
	before 1.2). Without a
	current point it is passed over, and after a `ClosePath` it starts a
	new contour, as `LineTo` does.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The caller's, as before.

   NOTES
	Without the memory for it the curve is not added, and the path
	remembers that something did not fit (see `MoveTo`).

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`QuadTo`, `LineTo`

   EXAMPLES
	    // A quarter of a circle of radius 100 round (0, 0).
	    vb.MoveTo(path, 100, 0);
	    vb.CubicTo(path, 100, 55.23, 55.23, 100, 0, 100);
vector.library/DisposeCanvas                      vector.library/DisposeCanvas

   NAME
	DisposeCanvas -- A canvas given back; its pens stay the caller's. Null does nothing.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn DisposeCanvas(vb: *VectorBase, canvas: ?*vector.Canvas) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -56.

   INPUTS
	- `canvas` - what `CreateCanvasTagList` answered, or null.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The canvas's working memory goes back, and any layer still pushed with
	it, not put down. The pens it drew on are left as they are, with what
	was drawn on them.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The canvas is gone: the handle must not be used again. The pens are
	the caller's to free.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`CreateCanvasTagList`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.DisposeCanvas(canvas);
vector.library/DisposePath                          vector.library/DisposePath

   NAME
	DisposePath -- A path given back. Null does nothing.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn DisposePath(vb: *VectorBase, path: ?*vector.Path) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -24.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - what `CreatePath` answered, or null.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The path's memory goes back, its segments with it. What was drawn
	with it stays on the pens it was drawn on.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The path is gone: the handle must not be used again.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`CreatePath`, `ClearPath`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.DisposePath(path);
vector.library/EndMask                                  vector.library/EndMask

   NAME
	EndMask -- The drawing since BeginMask made a mask (MASK_*) that clips what is drawn until PopClip.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn EndMask(vb: *VectorBase, canvas: *vector.Canvas, kind: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.3. LVO -116.

   INPUTS
	- `canvas` - the canvas.
	- `kind` - `MASK_ALPHA`, how much was drawn at each pixel;
	  `MASK_LUMINANCE`, that times the luminance of its colour - white
	  shows all, black nothing, as SVG's `mask`. With `MASK_INVERT`,
	  either the other way round.

   RESULT
	`VECTOR_OK`; `VECTORERR_NOMEM` when the mask's layer or the mask had
	no memory, when it clips nothing more than the clip it is in;
	`VECTORERR_DEPTH` past `MAX_CLIPS` clips one inside another, or with
	no mask begun - nothing is then pushed for it. Otherwise, whatever it
	answers, a `PopClip` goes with it.

   BEHAVIOR
	The layer `BeginMask` pushed is ended and given back; what was drawn
	on it becomes a clip, a byte a pixel - its coverage, or its
	luminance (0.2125 red, 0.7154 green, 0.0721 blue) times that - inside
	the clip pushed already on the layer under it. It is then pushed as
	`PushClip` pushes a path's: what is drawn from now on shows through
	it until the `PopClip`. Layers pushed inside the mask and not popped
	are popped first; the clips pushed inside it are taken off.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The mask is the canvas's, given back by the pop or by
	`DisposeCanvas`.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`BeginMask`, `PopClip`, `PushClip`

   EXAMPLES
	    _ = vb.BeginMask(canvas);
	    _ = vb.FillPath(canvas, letters, null, &white, vector.RULE_NONZERO);
	    _ = vb.EndMask(canvas, vector.MASK_ALPHA | vector.MASK_INVERT);
	    _ = vb.FillPath(canvas, plate, null, &grey, vector.RULE_NONZERO);
	    vb.PopClip(canvas);
vector.library/FillPath                                vector.library/FillPath

   NAME
	FillPath -- The path filled through the matrix (null: the identity) in the paint, by the rule (RULE_*).

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn FillPath(vb: *VectorBase, canvas: *vector.Canvas, path: *const vector.Path, matrix: ?*const vector.Matrix, paint: *const vector.Paint, rule: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -64.

   INPUTS
	- `canvas` - what it is drawn on.
	- `path` - what is filled. Every contour is closed for it, open or
	  not, with a straight line back to its start.
	- `matrix` - the path's units to pixels, or null when they are pixels.
	- `paint` - what it is painted in (`vector.Paint`): one colour, or
	  since 1.1 a linear or radial gradient with its stops, spread and
	  matrix (1.0 paints a gradient's colour), at the paint's opacity.
	  A gradient with no stops paints nothing, one with one paints its
	  colour, and one of no length or radius its last stop's colour, as
	  SVG has it.
	- `rule` - `RULE_NONZERO`: inside wherever the contours wind round a
	  point; `RULE_EVENODD`: wherever they wind round it an odd number of
	  times, so a contour inside another is a hole.

   RESULT
	`VECTOR_OK`, or `VECTORERR_NOMEM` when the path had run out of memory
	as it was built or there was no room to break it into lines: what
	there was is drawn.

   BEHAVIOR
	The curves are broken into lines at the size they are drawn at, and
	every pixel is painted by how much of it the shape covers, so the
	edges are smooth. The paint goes over what each pen held: a pen's
	share of the shape times the paint's opacity of it, mixed with the
	pen by its own coverage. Pixels off the canvas are passed over; so
	is everything with no pens set.

	A gradient is laid in its own units, taken into the path's by the
	paint's matrix and then to pixels by this one: each pixel's centre
	is taken back to see where along it the pixel is. Its colour there
	comes from 256 made from the stops for the fill, mixed as they are
	and not premultiplied. A radial gradient's focus outside its circle
	makes a cone, beside which nothing is painted, as SVG 2 draws it.

	With a layer pushed (`PushLayer`) it is drawn on the layer.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The path, the matrix and the paint are the caller's and only read;
	the pens are written.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`StrokePath`, `CreateCanvasTagList`, `MoveTo`, `PathBounds`, `PushLayer`

   EXAMPLES
	    const red = vector.Paint{ .colour = .{ .r = 1 } };
	    const twice = vector.Matrix.scale(2, 2);
	    _ = vb.FillPath(canvas, path, &twice, &red, vector.RULE_NONZERO);
vector.library/LineTo                                    vector.library/LineTo

   NAME
	LineTo -- A line from the current point to (x, y).

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn LineTo(vb: *VectorBase, path: *vector.Path, x: f32, y: f32) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -36.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path.
	- `x`, `y` - where the line ends, in the path's units.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	(x, y) is the current point from then on. Before the path's first
	`MoveTo` there is nowhere for a line to start, and it is passed over.
	After a `ClosePath` the line starts a new contour where the closed
	one began, as SVG's path data does.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The caller's, as before.

   NOTES
	Without the memory for it the line is not added, and the path
	remembers that something did not fit (see `MoveTo`).

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`MoveTo`, `QuadTo`, `CubicTo`, `ClosePath`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.MoveTo(path, 0, 0);
	    vb.LineTo(path, 100, 0);
	    vb.LineTo(path, 50, 80);
	    vb.ClosePath(path);
vector.library/MoveTo                                    vector.library/MoveTo

   NAME
	MoveTo -- A new contour started at (x, y).

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn MoveTo(vb: *VectorBase, path: *vector.Path, x: f32, y: f32) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -32.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path.
	- `x`, `y` - where the contour starts, in the path's units.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	A contour still open ends where it is: a fill closes it with a
	straight line, a stroke gives it its caps. (x, y) is the current
	point, where the next segment starts. A move followed by nothing is a
	contour of one point, which a stroke with round caps draws as a dot.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The caller's, as before.

   NOTES
	Without the memory for it the move is not added, and the path
	remembers that something did not fit: `FillPath` and `StrokePath`
	draw what there is and answer `VECTORERR_NOMEM`.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`LineTo`, `CubicTo`, `ClosePath`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.MoveTo(path, 10, 10);
	    vb.LineTo(path, 90, 10);
vector.library/PathBounds                            vector.library/PathBounds

   NAME
	PathBounds -- The bounds of the path's curves through the matrix (null: the identity); false for an empty path.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn PathBounds(vb: *VectorBase, path: *const vector.Path, matrix: ?*const vector.Matrix, bounds: *vector.Rect) bool

   SINCE
	1.1. LVO -88.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path.
	- `matrix` - the path's units to the ones the bounds are wanted in, or
	  null for the path's own.
	- `bounds` - where they are put.

   RESULT
	True and `bounds` filled in; false for a path with no point, and
	`bounds` left as it was.

   BEHAVIOR
	The smallest rectangle round the path's curves as drawn through the
	matrix - of the curves themselves, not of their control points -
	which is SVG's bounding box, the one `objectBoundingBox` gradients
	are laid over. Every point counts, a contour's move with the rest. A
	path with nothing but a point has bounds of no size.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The path and the matrix are the caller's and only read.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`FillPath`, `AddPathData`

   EXAMPLES
	    // A gradient laid over the shape's box, as objectBoundingBox has it.
	    var box: vector.Rect = undefined;
	    if (vb.PathBounds(path, null, &box)) {
	        paint.matrix = vector.Matrix.translate(box.x, box.y).then(vector.Matrix.scale(box.width, box.height));
	    }
vector.library/PopClip                                  vector.library/PopClip

   NAME
	PopClip -- The last clip or mask pushed taken off.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn PopClip(vb: *VectorBase, canvas: *vector.Canvas) void

   SINCE
	1.3. LVO -108.

   INPUTS
	- `canvas` - the canvas.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The clip pushed last, by `PushClip` or `EndMask`, is given back, and
	what is drawn from then on is clipped by the one it was in, if any.
	A clip that had no memory, or was past the most, is only ended. With
	no clip pushed it does nothing.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The clip's memory goes back to the canvas's.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`PushClip`, `EndMask`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.PopClip(canvas);
vector.library/PopLayer                                vector.library/PopLayer

   NAME
	PopLayer -- The last layer pushed put down on what is under it.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn PopLayer(vb: *VectorBase, canvas: *vector.Canvas) void

   SINCE
	1.1. LVO -96.

   INPUTS
	- `canvas` - the canvas.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	What was drawn on the layer goes down on what is under it - the layer
	it is inside, or the pens - at the layer's opacity, each pixel over
	what is there as a paint goes; and the layer is given back. A layer
	that had no memory, or was past the most, is only ended. With no
	layer pushed it does nothing.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The pens under the layer are written.

   NOTES
	Pens set with `SetCanvasPens` while a layer is pushed are the ones
	it goes down on.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`PushLayer`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.PopLayer(canvas);
vector.library/PushClip                                vector.library/PushClip

   NAME
	PushClip -- What is drawn until PopClip shows only inside the path, through the matrix (null: the identity), by the rule (RULE_*).

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn PushClip(vb: *VectorBase, canvas: *vector.Canvas, path: *const vector.Path, matrix: ?*const vector.Matrix, rule: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.3. LVO -104.

   INPUTS
	- `canvas` - the canvas.
	- `path` - the clip's outline, in its own units.
	- `matrix` - its units into pixels; null for the identity.
	- `rule` - `RULE_NONZERO` or `RULE_EVENODD`: which of its inside is
	  inside, as `FillPath` has it - SVG's `clip-rule`.

   RESULT
	`VECTOR_OK`; `VECTORERR_NOMEM` without the memory for the clip, which
	then clips nothing more than the clip it is in; `VECTORERR_DEPTH`
	past `MAX_CLIPS` clips one inside another, when it is nothing.
	Whatever it answers, a `PopClip` goes with it.

   BEHAVIOR
	The path's coverage is made, a byte a pixel, the edge smooth as a
	fill's is - inside the clip already pushed on this layer, if there is
	one. Every fill and stroke onto the layer drawn on now, and every
	layer put down on it, shows from then on only as much as the clip
	covers there, until the `PopClip` that goes with it.

	A layer pushed after the clip is not clipped while it is drawn on,
	only when it is put down: a group with its opacity on a layer goes
	through the clip as a whole. A clip pushed on a layer goes with it
	and is taken off by its `PopLayer` if not before.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The path and the matrix are the caller's and only read; the clip is
	the canvas's, given back by the pop or by `DisposeCanvas`.

   NOTES
	A clip is a byte a pixel of the whole canvas.

   BUGS
	An edge the clip and what is drawn through it share is drawn by the
	two coverages multiplied - a pixel half covered by both shows a
	quarter, not a half - so a shape clipped by its own outline has a
	fainter edge than unclipped. A browser draws it so; cairo, which cuts
	one outline by the other first, does not.

   SEE ALSO
	`PopClip`, `BeginMask`, `EndMask`, `PushLayer`, `FillPath`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.ClearPath(round);
	    vb.AddEllipse(round, 50, 50, 40, 40);
	    _ = vb.PushClip(canvas, round, null, vector.RULE_NONZERO);
	    _ = vb.FillPath(canvas, stripes, null, &red, vector.RULE_NONZERO);
	    vb.PopClip(canvas);
vector.library/PushLayer                              vector.library/PushLayer

   NAME
	PushLayer -- What is drawn until PopLayer goes onto a layer of its own, put down at the opacity.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn PushLayer(vb: *VectorBase, canvas: *vector.Canvas, opacity: f32) i32

   SINCE
	1.1. LVO -92.

   INPUTS
	- `canvas` - the canvas.
	- `opacity` - how much of the layer shows when it is put down, 0 to 1:
	  SVG's `opacity` on a group.

   RESULT
	`VECTOR_OK`; `VECTORERR_NOMEM` without the memory for the layer, which
	is then drawn without one; `VECTORERR_DEPTH` past `MAX_LAYERS` layers
	one inside another, when the layer is nothing. Whatever it answers,
	a `PopLayer` goes with it.

   BEHAVIOR
	Every fill and stroke from now until the `PopLayer` that goes with
	it is drawn onto pens of the layer's own, clear and the canvas's
	size; the pop puts them down on what is under them at `opacity`. So a
	group's shapes that overlap are seen as one, at the group's opacity,
	not through each other. Layers go inside one another.

	Without the memory for the layer, the opacity goes into every paint
	drawn until the pop instead, which is the same where the shapes do
	not overlap - drawn a little wrong rather than not at all.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The layer is the canvas's, given back by the pop or by
	`DisposeCanvas`.

   NOTES
	A layer is four bytes a pixel of the whole canvas, cleared when
	pushed: on a small machine, a large canvas's layers are what runs
	out first.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`PopLayer`, `FillPath`

   EXAMPLES
	    _ = vb.PushLayer(canvas, 0.5);
	    _ = vb.FillPath(canvas, body, null, &grey, vector.RULE_NONZERO);
	    _ = vb.StrokePath(canvas, body, null, &black, &outline);
	    vb.PopLayer(canvas);
vector.library/QuadTo                                    vector.library/QuadTo

   NAME
	QuadTo -- A quadratic curve from the current point to (x, y), pulled toward (x1, y1).

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn QuadTo(vb: *VectorBase, path: *vector.Path, x1: f32, y1: f32, x: f32, y: f32) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -40.

   INPUTS
	- `path` - the path.
	- `x1`, `y1` - the control point, in the path's units.
	- `x`, `y` - where the curve ends.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The curve leaves the current point toward (x1, y1) and arrives at
	(x, y) from it, as TrueType's and SVG's `Q` curves do; (x, y) is the
	current point from then on. It is kept as a curve and broken into
	lines only when drawn. Without a current point it is passed over, and
	after a `ClosePath` it starts a new contour, as `LineTo` does.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The caller's, as before.

   NOTES
	Without the memory for it the curve is not added, and the path
	remembers that something did not fit (see `MoveTo`).

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`CubicTo`, `LineTo`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.MoveTo(path, 0, 50);
	    vb.QuadTo(path, 50, 0, 100, 50);
vector.library/SetCanvasPens                      vector.library/SetCanvasPens

   NAME
	SetCanvasPens -- The pens the canvas draws on from now on; null draws nothing.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn SetCanvasPens(vb: *VectorBase, canvas: *vector.Canvas, pens: ?[*]graphics.Pen, bytes_per_row: u32) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -60.

   INPUTS
	- `canvas` - the canvas.
	- `pens` - its width by its height of them, 0xAARRGGBB, or null.
	- `bytes_per_row` - from one row of them to the next; 0 for four
	  times the canvas's width.

   RESULT
	Nothing.

   BEHAVIOR
	The canvas keeps its size and its working memory, and draws on these
	pens instead: a program that draws frame after frame into buffers of
	one size hands each over in turn. With null, a fill or a stroke
	draws nothing.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The pens stay the caller's, and must last while they are drawn on.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`CreateCanvasTagList`, `FillPath`

   EXAMPLES
	    vb.SetCanvasPens(canvas, frame.pens, frame.bytes_per_row);
vector.library/StrokePath                            vector.library/StrokePath

   NAME
	StrokePath -- The path stroked in its own units, then through the matrix (null: the identity), in the paint.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn StrokePath(vb: *VectorBase, canvas: *vector.Canvas, path: *const vector.Path, matrix: ?*const vector.Matrix, paint: *const vector.Paint, stroke: *const vector.Stroke) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -68.

   INPUTS
	- `canvas` - what it is drawn on.
	- `path` - what is stroked: its open contours with caps at their
	  ends, its closed ones joined all round.
	- `matrix` - the path's units to pixels, or null when they are pixels.
	- `paint` - what it is painted in, as `FillPath` paints.
	- `stroke` - how (`vector.Stroke`), in the path's own units: the
	  width, the joins (`JOIN_MITER`, `JOIN_ROUND`, `JOIN_BEVEL`), the caps
	  (`CAP_BUTT`, `CAP_ROUND`, `CAP_SQUARE`), the miter limit, in stroke
	  widths, and since 1.1 the dashes (1.0 draws a dashed stroke whole).

   RESULT
	`VECTOR_OK`, or `VECTORERR_NOMEM` as `FillPath` answers it.

   BEHAVIOR
	The stroke is made of pieces in the path's units - one a line, the
	corner's on its outer side, a cap at each open end - and those are
	drawn through the matrix, so a stroke drawn through one that stretches
	a way is stretched with it, as SVG draws one. A miter longer than the
	limit is bevelled. Where a curve was broken into lines, they are
	joined round whatever the join, as the curve turns. A contour of one
	point is a dot when the caps are round, and nothing otherwise; a
	stroke under a fiftieth of a pixel wide is not drawn.

	Since 1.2 the pieces meet edge to edge: a line's is cut on the inside
	of a corner where the next one's begins, so no pixel at the stroke's
	edge is painted by two (1.1's overlapped there, and came out a little
	heavy). A corner whose lines are too short for that - a curve tighter
	than the stroke is wide, a sharp turn between short lines - still
	overlaps.

	Dashes are cut from each contour in the path's units, from
	`dash_offset` along it, before it is stroked, so each dash has its
	caps; they go on from one segment to the next and round a closed
	contour, whose last dash is joined to its first. An odd count of
	lengths is gone through twice, and lengths that are negative or come
	to nothing draw the stroke whole, as SVG has it. A stroke is cut at
	most 65536 times; past that it goes on as the dash or gap it is in.

	With a layer pushed (`PushLayer`) it is drawn on the layer.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none needed.
	- Process: a Task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The path, the matrix, the paint and the stroke are the caller's and
	only read; the pens are written.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`FillPath`, `ClosePath`

   EXAMPLES
	    const black = vector.Paint{};
	    const line = vector.Stroke{ .width = 2, .join = vector.JOIN_ROUND, .cap = vector.CAP_ROUND };
	    _ = vb.StrokePath(canvas, path, null, &black, &line);
